# Keep local secrets out of the deployment bundle.
#
# `vercel deploy` uploads the working directory, and .gitignore does not govern
# that. So .env was being shipped into the build container -- the CLI said so on
# every deploy ("Detected .env file, it is strongly recommended to use Vercel's
# env handling instead"), and Next.js then loaded it at build time.
#
# Nothing was ever served: /.env, /.env.local and /.env.example all return 404,
# because only files under public/ are reachable. The real problem is a second,
# unmanaged copy of the configuration living in build artifacts, free to drift
# out of sync with the encrypted values that `vercel env ls` reports.
#
# All five variables now live in the project's environment (Production), so the
# file has no job left to do here.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Never needed remotely: the build regenerates them.
.next
node_modules
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# Local tooling and scratch output.
.bug-hunter
